[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Netcode for GameObjects (NGO) & Client-Server Architecture in Unity"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Netcode for GameObjects (NGO) is Unity\'s official high-level networking library designed for building multiplayer games. In this tutorial, we will explore the fundamentals of Client-Server architecture using NGO, configure a network manager, and write a basic authoritative synchronization script."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Setup"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unity 2022.3 LTS or newer."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Netcodes for GameObjects package installed via the Unity Package Manager."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Basic understanding of C# and Unity Component workflows."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 1: Setting up the NetworkManager"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The NetworkManager is the core singleton that coordinates all networking operations in NGO. Create an empty GameObject in your scene named "NetworkManager", and add the "NetworkManager" component to it. Additionally, add the "Unity Transport" component to handle transport-layer protocols."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 2: Creating a Networked Player Prefab"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To synchronize objects across the network, they must have a NetworkObject component attached. Create a simple Capsule GameObject, add a "NetworkObject" component, and register this prefab inside the NetworkManager\'s "NetworkPrefabs" list."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 3: Writing the Player Movement Script"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"In a Client-Server architecture, player inputs are captured on the client, sent to the server for validation or processing, and then replicated back to all connected clients. Create a script named PlayerController.cs and attach it to your networked player prefab."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"using Unity.Netcode;
using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerController : NetworkBehaviour
{
    public float moveSpeed = 5f;

    private void Update()
    {
        if (!IsOwner) return;

        float moveX = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
        float moveZ = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");

        Vector3 moveDir = new Vector3(moveX, 0f, moveZ);
        transform.position += moveDir * moveSpeed * Time.deltaTime;
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Notice the use of the "IsOwner" check. This ensures that only the client who owns this specific NetworkObject instance can process local inputs and move their character locally."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Step 4: Testing the Build"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a simple UI with buttons to trigger NetworkManager.Singleton.StartHost() and NetworkManager.Singleton.StartClient()."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Build the project or use ParrelSync to test multiple instances running simultaneously on your local machine."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Verify that spawning a Host spawns the player prefab, and connecting a Client correctly instantiates and synchronizes the second player."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"You have successfully configured Netcode for GameObjects, established a basic NetworkManager setup, and implemented owner-based client movement. For production-ready games, consider utilizing ServerRpc and ClientRpc methods for authoritative gameplay validation."}]
